  <section id="chto-eto">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>The common peroneal (fibular) nerve is a branch of the sciatic nerve. Below the knee it winds around the head of the fibula and passes through a narrow fibrous canal. The nerve lifts the foot and toes and supplies sensation to the outer shin and the top of the foot.</p>
    <NerveMap limb="leg" focus="peroneal" lang="en" />
    <h3>Causes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>a habit of sitting with crossed legs;</li>
      <li>long work squatting or kneeling;</li>
      <li>rapid weight loss — the fat "cushion" protecting the nerve disappears;</li>
      <li>a tight cast, brace or high boots;</li>
      <li>long immobility — after surgery or in intensive care;</li>
      <li>knee injuries, fibula fractures, cysts around the knee.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="simptomy">
    <h2>Symptoms</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Foot drop</strong>: hard to lift the toes, the person catches the floor and lifts the knee high — a "steppage", slapping gait;</li>
      <li>numbness of the outer shin and the top of the foot;</li>
      <li>weakness when trying to stand on the heels;</li>
      <li>there is usually no back pain.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnostika">
    <h2>Diagnosis</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Examination</strong> — foot muscle strength, the area of numbness, tapping over the nerve at the fibular head.</li>
      <li><strong>Nerve conduction study</strong> — confirms that the nerve is affected at the knee and shows the degree of damage.</li>
      <li><strong>Nerve ultrasound</strong> and, if needed, <strong>knee MRI</strong> — cysts and other masses.</li>
      <li><strong>Lumbar spine MRI</strong> — if a disc herniation needs to be ruled out.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="lechenie">
    <h2>Treatment</h2>
    <p><strong>Without surgery</strong> — remove the cause: stop crossing your legs and squatting, loosen the cast or brace. During recovery — a brace holding up the foot. Exercise therapy, medication as prescribed by a doctor.</p>
    <p><strong>Surgery</strong> — if the nerve is compressed by a cyst or another mass, if there is no improvement for several months or weakness is getting worse. Through an incision below the knee the nerve is exposed and freed from its canal. After surgery — a brace during recovery and exercise therapy supervised by a physiotherapist.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="srochno" slot="urgent">
    <h2>When to seek urgent help</h2>
    <div class="callout alert">
      <p>Foot drop together with severe back pain, numbness in the groin area or problems passing urine — <strong>call emergency services immediately (112; 103 in Russia)</strong>: this may be nerve compression in the spine. If the foot stopped lifting after a knee injury — go to an emergency department the same day.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Article>
